    <article class="doc">
        <h2 id="services">Manage services</h2>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Command</th>
                    <th>Purpose</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl status name.service</code></td>
                    <td>Show unit status and recent journal entries.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl start name.service</code></td>
                    <td>Start now; does not enable the service at boot.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl stop name.service</code></td>
                    <td>Stop now.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl restart name.service</code></td>
                    <td>Stop and start again.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl reload name.service</code></td>
                    <td>Ask the service to reload; works only if its unit supports reload.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl enable name.service</code></td>
                    <td>Configure the unit to be started through its install target.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl enable --now name.service</code></td>
                    <td>Enable and start.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl disable --now name.service</code></td>
                    <td>Disable and stop.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>systemctl list-units --type=service --all</code></td>
                    <td>List loaded service units, including inactive ones.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
            Unit names commonly end in <code>.service</code>; systemctl can
            often infer the suffix. Enabling a unit does not guarantee it will
            start: dependencies, conditions, or a failed unit can prevent that.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sysusers">Create a service account with sysusers.d</h2>

        <p>
            Define a system user in <code>/etc/sysusers.d/example.conf</code>.
            The <code>u</code> record creates the user; <code>-</code> requests
            an automatically allocated ID and leaves the optional home and
            shell fields unset.
        </p>

        <pre><code># Type  Name     ID  GECOS                    Home  Shell
u       example  -   "Example service account" -     -</code></pre>

        <p>
            Process the file immediately with <code>systemd-sysusers</code>.
            The system also processes sysusers.d files during boot. The unit's
            <code>User=example</code> and <code>Group=example</code> settings
            require the account and group to exist.
        </p>

        <pre><code>systemd-sysusers /etc/sysusers.d/example.conf
getent passwd example
getent group example</code></pre>

        <h2 id="tmpfiles">Create directories with tmpfiles.d</h2>

        <p>
            Use <code>/etc/tmpfiles.d/example.conf</code> to declare a
            directory and its ownership. This example creates persistent
            application state under <code>/var/lib</code>:
        </p>

        <pre><code># Type  Path              Mode  User     Group    Age  Argument
d       /var/lib/example  0750  example  example  -    -</code></pre>

        <p>
            Apply the file now with <code>systemd-tmpfiles</code>. The system
            also processes tmpfiles.d rules during boot. For a service-specific
            runtime directory under <code>/run</code>, prefer the unit's
            <code>RuntimeDirectory=example</code> setting; systemd manages that
            directory for the unit.
        </p>

        <pre><code>systemd-tmpfiles --create /etc/tmpfiles.d/example.conf
stat -c '%U:%G %a %n' /var/lib/example</code></pre>

        <h2 id="unit">Write a service unit</h2>

        <p>
            Create <code>/etc/systemd/system/example.service</code> for a local
            system unit. Replace the executable path and user with values that
            exist on the host.
        </p>

        <pre><code>[Unit]
Description=Example service

[Service]
Type=exec
User=example
Group=example
ExecStart=/usr/bin/exampled --config /etc/example/config.toml
Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>

        <p>
            <code>Type=exec</code> is supported by systemd 240 and later. On
            older versions, use <code>Type=simple</code> when appropriate.
            <code>WantedBy=multi-user.target</code> only defines the install
            relationship; it does not enable the unit by itself.
        </p>

        <pre><code>systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/example.service
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl start example.service
systemctl status example.service
systemctl enable example.service</code></pre>

        <p>
            Run <code>daemon-reload</code> after adding or changing unit files.
            Use <code>systemctl edit example.service</code> to create an override
            instead of editing a vendor unit under <code>/usr/lib</code> or
            <code>/lib</code>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="template">Template units and instances</h2>

        <p>
            A template unit has an <code>@</code> before its suffix, for example
            <code>worker@.service</code>. Starting a unit with an instance name
            creates a distinct instance: <code>worker@alpha.service</code>.
            In the unit file, <code>%i</code> expands to the instance name.
        </p>

        <p>
            Save this as <code>/etc/systemd/system/worker@.service</code>:
        </p>

        <pre><code>[Unit]
Description=Worker instance %i

[Service]
Type=exec
ExecStart=/usr/bin/worker --config /etc/worker/%i.conf
Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>

        <p>
            Reload systemd after creating the template, then start or enable
            named instances. Each instance needs its own configuration file.
        </p>

        <pre><code>systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl start worker@alpha.service
systemctl start worker@beta.service
systemctl status worker@alpha.service
systemctl enable --now worker@alpha.service
systemctl list-units 'worker@*.service'</code></pre>

        <p>
            <code>systemctl enable --now worker@alpha.service</code> enables
            only the <code>alpha</code> instance for future starts; repeat for
            each instance that should start at boot. Use <code>%I</code> instead
            of <code>%i</code> when the unescaped instance string is needed.
        </p>

        <h2 id="inspect-logs">Inspect logs</h2>

        <pre><code>journalctl -u example.service -b
journalctl -u example.service -f
journalctl -u 'worker@alpha.service' -b
journalctl -p err -b</code></pre>

        <p>
            <code>-b</code> limits entries to this boot; <code>-f</code>
            follows new entries. Journal retention and persistence depend on
            host configuration.
        </p>

        <h2 id="targets">Targets</h2>

        <p>
            Targets group units and express system states. Inspect target
            relationships and the default target with:
        </p>

        <pre><code>systemctl get-default
systemctl list-units --type=target
systemctl list-dependencies multi-user.target</code></pre>
    </article>
